<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Thessaly</h1><p class="st-meta">1268 CE – 1318 CE (approx.) · capital Neopatras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek principality in Thessaly ruled by an offshoot of the Epirote dynasty, independent of Byzantium until the Catalans and Byzantines divided it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-epirus">Despotate of Epirus</a> around 1268, his illegitimate son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-doukas">John I Doukas</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a>. Byzantine writers called the region Great Vlachia, after its many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlach</a> inhabitants. John resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>, defeating a Byzantine army at Neopatras around 1275.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>John I held the Byzantine title of sebastokrator and ruled a mixed Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlach</a> population of farmers and herders through local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>. He allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-athens">Duchy of Athens</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a> against the restored <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. His sons Constantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-doukas">John II</a> continued his policies, John II marrying a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-doukas">John II</a> died without heirs in 1318, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> broke apart. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a>, ruling the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-athens">Duchy of Athens</a>, seized the south and created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-neopatras">Duchy of Neopatras</a>, while local lords and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> took the north.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-thessaly.jpg" alt="Principality of Thessaly" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: William Robert Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ShepherdByzempire1265.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1268 CE – 1318 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Neopatras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John I Doukas, Constantine Doukas, John II Doukas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Medieval Greek, Aromanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Vlachia, Duchy of Neopatras (Greek)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1293&amp;focus=principality-of-thessaly" aria-label="Principality of Thessaly on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5200 753 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Thessaly lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5519 1030h0M5554 1046h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5520 1009h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5520 1009h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=1293&amp;focus=principality-of-thessaly">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
